---
import Journey from '../../layouts/Journey.astro';
import Step from '../../components/journey/Step.astro';
import Next from '../../components/journey/Next.astro';
import More from '../../components/journey/More.astro';
import ForLevel from '../../components/level/ForLevel.astro';
import Fact from '../../components/data/Fact.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/nav';
import Verified from '../../components/data/Verified.astro';
import { models } from '../../data/models';

const sizes = new Map<number, string[]>();
const ids: string[] = [];
for (const [key, m] of Object.entries(models) as [string, any][]) {
  if (!m.current) continue;
  for (const [field, f] of [['context', m.context], ['harnessContext', m.harnessContext]] as const) {
    if (!f) continue;
    const k = Math.round(f.value / 1000);
    sizes.set(k, [...(sizes.get(k) ?? []), field === 'context' ? m.name : `${m.name} in its own harness`]);
    ids.push(`models.${key}.${field}`);
  }
}
const windows = [...sizes].sort((a, b) => b[0] - a[0]).map(([k, names]) => ({ k, label: `${k >= 1000 ? k / 1000 + 'M' : k + 'k'} tokens`, names }));
---
<Journey chapter="window" first="pile" title="The window fills (and rots)" description="Everything an agent reads stays in its window. Long before a big window hits 100%, unrelated files and old logs rot attention and produce worse output.">
  <Step id="pile" title="Nothing leaves the window">
    <p><a href={url('journey/loop/')}>The fix from step 6</a> is all still in the window: your message, every file it read, every command output. All of it is read again on every turn.</p>
  </Step>

  <Step id="rot" title="Why bigger windows still get worse: context rot">
    <p>A common myth is that a huge 1M-token window means you can dump everything into one endless chat. Switch the mix below—notice the window is nowhere near full, yet quality collapses:</p>
    <div class="pick" role="group" aria-label="Window cleanliness">
      <button type="button" data-do="rot" data-val="focused" data-default>Focused (12% full)</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="rot" data-val="cluttered">Cluttered (45% full)</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="rot" data-val="rotted">Rotted (70% full)</button>
    </div>
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p>Behind the scenes, the model weighs every word in the window when picking its next answer. Fill a big window with three old tasks, failed attempts and unrelated files, and your real instructions get drowned out—the answers get lazier, miss rules you gave earlier, and repeat old mistakes.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="technical">
      <p>Behind the scenes, transformer attention has to spread across every token in the window. Stale debugging logs, dead-end diffs and unrelated files act as distractors (<strong>context rot</strong>): long before hitting the token limit, the model misses type constraints, forgets earlier rules and invents workarounds.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer">
      <p>Nominal context capacity (200k–1M tokens) is an upper bound on input length, not on reasoning reliability. Attention dilution, lost-in-the-middle recall drop, and conflicting prior tool traces cause measurable degradation as unrelated token mass grows—keep each thread scoped to one clean task.</p>
    </ForLevel>
  </Step>

  <Step id="long" title="Keep working: scale vs cap">
    <p>Drag to run the session longer. Pick a smaller window and the same work hits the rot zone—and then the hard wall—much sooner.</p>
    <div class="controls">
      <label class="control"><span>In the window <output>150k</output></span>
        <input type="range" min="0" max="1000" step="25" value="150" data-bind="used" data-unit="k" />
      </label>
      <label class="control"><span>Window</span>
        <select data-bind="cap">
          {windows.map((w) => <option value={w.k}>{w.label}</option>)}
        </select>
      </label>
    </div>
  </Step>

  <Step id="compact" title="Two ways to clear the fog">
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p><strong>Compact</strong> replaces the messy history with a short summary so you can keep going on the same task. <strong>New thread</strong> wipes the slate completely clean—almost always better when you finish one job and start the next.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="technical">
      <p>Use <strong>Compact</strong> only mid-task when you cannot restart yet (every compaction loses subtle detail). When switching tasks or after a long debugging spiral, commit your working code and open a <strong>New thread</strong> with a crisp 3-line brief.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is="engineer">
      <p>Compaction trades hard overflow for lossy summary compression. For repeatable quality, treat git commits + concise handoff specs as explicit state checkpoints and spin up fresh windows at task boundaries.</p>
    </ForLevel>
    <div class="row">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-do="compact">Compact</button>
      <button class="btn ghost" type="button" data-do="fresh">New thread</button>
      <button class="btn ghost" type="button" data-do="reset">Undo</button>
    </div>
    <More title="What Claude Code keeps">
      <p>After compaction: <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.compactionKeeps" />. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.compactionReread" show="claim" /></p>
      <p><Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.autoCompactAt" show="claim" /></p>
    </More>
  </Step>

  <Step id="paste" title="One giant paste">
    <p>Paste one unfiltered 200k-token log file and watch it crowd the window—and rot attention—on every turn that follows.</p>
    <div class="row">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-do="paste">Paste a long log</button>
      <button class="btn ghost" type="button" data-do="reset">Undo</button>
    </div>
    <Next from="window" more={[{ href: 'wiki/compaction/', text: 'How compaction works', side: true }, { href: 'journey/meter/#cache', text: 'How window size affects cost' }]} />
  </Step>
  <Fragment slot="foot">
    <p>Window sizes: {windows.map((w) => `${w.label} (${w.names.join(', ')})`).join('; ')}. The stack is drawn to scale against the window you pick; each block represents 25k tokens.</p>
    <Verified ids={ids} />
  </Fragment>
</Journey>
